Q: Check if String only contains latin letters including accents A: <p><a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/regex/Pattern.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/util/regex/Pattern.html</a>
Java's Pattern supports Unicode scripts through <code>\p{...}</code>, including latin script matching.
Use Unicode properties instead of [a-zA-Z]. In Java, \p{IsLatin} matches Latin-script characters, and \p{L} restricts the match to letters. Intersecting both gives Latin letters, including accented letters. Normalize the string first if you want composed and decomposed accented characters to be handled consistently.</p>
<pre><code>import java.text.Normalizer;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class Main {
    private static final Pattern LATIN_LETTERS =
            Pattern.compile(&quot;^[\\p{IsLatin}&amp;&amp;\\p{L}]+$&quot;);

    public static boolean isOnlyLatinLetters(String input) {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }

        String normalized = Normalizer.normalize(input, Normalizer.Form.NFC);
        return LATIN_LETTERS.matcher(normalized).matches();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isOnlyLatinLetters(&quot;Jose&quot;));      // true
        System.out.println(isOnlyLatinLetters(&quot;José&quot;));      // true
        System.out.println(isOnlyLatinLetters(&quot;João&quot;));      // true
        System.out.println(isOnlyLatinLetters(&quot;John1234&quot;));   // false
        System.out.println(isOnlyLatinLetters(&quot;John Doe&quot;));  // false
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><code>&quot;^[\\p{IsLatin}&amp;&amp;\\p{L}]+$&quot;</code> meaning:</p>
<pre><code>\p{IsLatin}: Latin script characters
\p{L}: letters only
&amp;&amp;: intersection, so it must be both Latin and a letter
</code></pre>
